<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shivaji of Thanjavur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1855">1855</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shivaji was the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> raja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfoji-ii">Serfoji II</a>, ruling from 1832 to 1855 with the administration already in British hands. When he died in 1855 without a male heir, the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> annexed Thanjavur under the doctrine of lapse. The palace and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Saraswati</a> Mahal library remained with the royal family.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shivaji-of-thanjavur.jpg" alt="Shivaji of Thanjavur" width="205" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Royal Ark · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Choladesadhipati_Srimant_Rajasri_Maharaja_Kshatrapati_Sri_Shivaji_Raje_Sahib_Bhonsle_Chhatrapati_Maharaj.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Shivaji, of the Bhonsle dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a>, was the only surviving son of Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfoji-ii">Serfoji II</a> when his father died in 1832, and he ruled the fortress of Thanjavur and its surroundings from then until 1855. The missionary Heber described the young Shivaji as a pale and sickly child, but his health seems to have improved as he grew, and he became known for his physical and mental attainments. He enlarged the Saraswathi Mahal Library and gave it many useful books, and a scholar named Varahappaiyar prepared a catalogue of all its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>.</p><p>He is best remembered for the episode known as the arrest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchi</a> mutt. In 1843–44 the earrings, or tatankas, of the goddess Akhilandeswari in the Jambukeshwarar Temple were replaced, and the Kanchi mutt, then based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbakonam">Kumbakonam</a>, moved with all its retinue to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Trichy</a> to consecrate them. A lawsuit delayed the ceremony, and the court case and rites left the mutt so deep in debt that it could not go back to Kumbakonam. Its administrator asked Shivaji to let the retinue stop at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> to receive donations, and the raja firmly refused.</p><p>Yet as the palanquin of the Shankaracharya and his retinue made their way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbakonam">Kumbakonam</a>, the raja's sepoys stopped them on the banks of the Cauvery at Thiruvaiyaru, surrounded them and respectfully escorted them into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a>, where Shivaji and the citizens gave them a royal reception. It was later said that Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> had appeared to the raja in a dream a few nights earlier and ordered him to honour the mutt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shivaji died on 29 October 1855. Because there was no legitimate heir to the throne, the British East India Company annexed the kingdom under the doctrine of lapse, and he was the last raja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> known to have wielded any authority.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shivaji of Thanjavur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When <b>Shivaji II</b> died in 1855 without a male heir, the British annexed Thanjavur under the doctrine of lapse.</blockquote><a href="/state/thanjavur-maratha-kingdom">Thanjavur Maratha Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
